{"name":"Light Imagine","hex":"#aed4d8","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"light","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye (about 400–750 nanometers): visible light.","Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye or in nearby ranges (infrared or ultraviolet radiation)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To start (a fire).","To set fire to; to set burning."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/light"},{"term":"imagine","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To form a mental image of something; to envision or create something in one's mind.","To believe in something created by one's own mind, often something false."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A short fanfic or prompt placing a reader insert in a novel scenario with a character or celebrity."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/imagine"}],"entries":[{"title":"Imagine (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Imagine\" is a song by the English musician John Lennon from his 1971 album of the same name. The lyrics encourage listeners to imagine a world of peace, beginning with living without religion and its concepts, without economic materialism, and without borders separating nations. Shortly before his death, Lennon said that much of the song's lyrics and content came from his wife, Yoko Ono; in 2017, she received credit as a co-writer."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/2/2a/Lennon_Imagine_Sleeve_1975.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Imagine_(song)","wikidata":"Q1971"},{"title":"Light","blocks":[{"text":"Light, visible light, or visible radiation is electromagnetic radiation that can be perceived by the human eye. Visible light spans the visible spectrum and is usually defined as having wavelengths in the range of 400–700 nanometers (nm), corresponding to frequencies of 750–420 terahertz. The visible band sits adjacent to the infrared and the ultraviolet, called collectively optical radiation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Light","wikidata":"Q9128"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/One_of_John_Lennon%27s_Steinway_pianos.jpg/500px-One_of_John_Lennon%27s_Steinway_pianos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/One_of_John_Lennon%27s_Steinway_pianos.jpg/1280px-One_of_John_Lennon%27s_Steinway_pianos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/One_of_John_Lennon%27s_Steinway_pianos.jpg/500px-One_of_John_Lennon%27s_Steinway_pianos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Lennon's Steinway piano, on which he composed \"Imagine\", was bought by singer George Michael in 2000 to keep it in the UK.[6]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOne_of_John_Lennon's_Steinway_pianos.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/John_Lennon_Imagine_1971.jpg/500px-John_Lennon_Imagine_1971.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/John_Lennon_Imagine_1971.jpg/500px-John_Lennon_Imagine_1971.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A 1971 Billboard advertisement for \"Imagine\"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJohn_Lennon_Imagine_1971.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/John_Lennon_Peace_Monument_-_PEACE_ON_EARTH_-_October_9th